.TH GETTY 8 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
getty \- set up a terminal line and prompt for a login name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B getty
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR login-program ]
.I tty
.RI [ speed
.RI [ type
.RI [ linedisc ]]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B getty
opens a terminal line, makes it the controlling terminal, prints a banner and a
.B login:
prompt, reads a user name and executes
.BR login (1)
with it.
It is normally started by
.BR init (8)
on
.IR /dev/tty1 ,
.I /dev/tty2
and
.IR /dev/tty3 ,
and respawned by
.B init
whenever it exits.
.PP
.I tty
is the terminal device.
A name that does not begin with
.B /
has
.I /dev/
prepended.
.PP
The line is set up as follows:
.IP 1. 3
Standard input, output and error are closed and
.I tty
is opened read-write without becoming the controlling terminal.
If it cannot be opened, a message is written to
.I /dev/console
and
.B getty
exits.
.IP 2. 3
.BR setsid (2)
is called (a failure is ignored) and the line is made the controlling
terminal with
.BR TIOCSCTTY .
It is then duplicated onto descriptors 0, 1 and 2.
.IP 3. 3
If the line is a terminal, its modes are reset: input
.BR BRKINT ,
.BR ICRNL ,
.BR IXON ;
output
.BR OPOST ,
.BR ONLCR ;
control
.BR CS8 ,
.BR CREAD ,
.BR HUPCL ,
.B CLOCAL
(other control bits kept); local
.BR ISIG ,
.BR ICANON ,
.BR ECHO ,
.BR ECHOE ,
.BR ECHOK ,
.BR ECHOCTL ,
.BR ECHOKE ,
.BR IEXTEN .
The special characters are set to EOF ^D, ERASE DEL (0x7f), INTR ^C,
KILL ^U, QUIT ^\e, SUSP ^Z, START ^Q, STOP ^S, with MIN 1 and TIME 0.
The line speed is not changed.
.IP 4. 3
Unless
.B \-h
or
.B \-i
is given,
.I /etc/issue
is copied to the terminal with the escapes described below expanded.
If
.I /etc/issue
does not exist, a short banner naming the host and line is printed instead.
.IP 5. 3
Unless
.B \-i
is given,
.B login:
is printed and a user name is read, up to a newline or carriage return
(at most 63 characters).
Backspace and DEL delete the previous character.
Only letters, digits and the characters
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
.B \-
and
.B $
are kept; any other byte is silently discarded.
The program is then executed as
.IR "login-program user" .
With
.BR \-i ,
it is executed with no arguments.
.PP
If the name read is empty, or end of file or an error is read,
.B getty
exits 0 and
.B init
starts a new one.
.SS /etc/issue escapes
.TP
.B \een
newline
.TP
.B \ees
operating system name from
.BR uname (2)
(Substrate if that fails)
.TP
.B \eeh
host name, from
.BR gethostname (2),
else the first line of
.IR /etc/hostname ,
else
.B substrate
.TP
.B \eer
kernel release from
.BR uname (2)
.TP
.B \eel
the
.I tty
argument as given
.TP
.B \eed
current date,
.I YYYY-MM-DD
.TP
.B \eet
current time,
.I HH:MM:SS
.TP
.B \eeu
always 0
.TP
.B \ee\e\e
a backslash
.PP
Any other backslash sequence is printed unchanged.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h
Do not print
.I /etc/issue
or the fallback banner.
.TP
.B \-i
Do not prompt: execute the login program with no arguments and let it
prompt for the name.
.TP
.BI \-l " login-program"
Execute
.I login-program
instead of
.IR /bin/login .
.TP
.IR speed ", " type ", " linedisc
Accepted for compatibility with BSD
.BR getty ;
ignored.
.PP
Any other argument beginning with
.B \-
(including
.BR \-\-help )
prints a usage message.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B getty
does not return when the login program is executed successfully.
Otherwise:
.TP
.B 0
End of file, an error, or an empty name was read at the prompt.
.TP
.B 1
The line could not be opened or duplicated, or the login program could not
be executed.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/issue
Pre-login banner.
.TP
.I /etc/hostname
Host name fallback for
.BR \eeh .
.TP
.I /bin/login
Default login program.
.TP
.I /bin/getty
A placeholder built from
.I bin/getty
in the source tree: it does nothing and exits 0.
The working program is
.IR /sbin/getty ,
which is the one
.BR init (8)
runs.
.SH EXAMPLE
Run a login prompt on the second virtual terminal without the banner:
.EX
# /sbin/getty \-h tty2
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR login (1),
.BR init (8),
.BR telnetd (8),
.BR sdm (8),
.BR stty (1),
.BR tty (1),
.BR termios (3)
.SH BUGS
There is no
.I gettytab
or
.IR inittab ;
line speed and parity are never set.
.PP
The
.B \eeu
escape always prints 0 rather than the number of logged-in users.
